The PROLED <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> models continue to offer up to 24 hours of battery life despite the switch to a new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> display and touch capabilities.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/macbook-pro-proled-touch.jpg" alt="" width="2260" height="1271" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1074117" /><br/>
Both the PROLED and the mini-LED 14-inch and 16-inch &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models offer the same maximum battery life estimates.<br/>
<ul><br/>
 	<li><strong>16-inch, M5 Max</strong> - Up to 22 hours streaming video, up to 16 hours web</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>16-inch, M5 Pro</strong> - Up to 24 hours streaming video, up to 17 hours web</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>14-inch, M5 Max</strong> - Up to 20 hours streaming video, up to 13 hours web</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>14-inch, M5 Pro</strong> - Up to 22 hours streaming video, up to 14 hours web</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
The mini-LED and PROLED models have the same M5 Pro and M5 Max chips inside, so the display is the main difference between them. The &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display's efficiency likely offsets the power used by the touch integration. The 14-inch PROLED and mini-LED models have a 72.4-watt-hour battery, while the 16-inch models have a 100-watt-hour battery.<br/>
